1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,324
This is Feelable. Every diagram, feelable.

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:09,008
Image AI makes amazing pictures. But only for people who can see.

3
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:12,255
So we asked: who does image AI leave out?

4
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:20,286
Blind students. Every diagram in their textbooks is remade by hand. It takes hours, and they wait weeks.

5
00:00:20,833 --> 00:00:24,825
So Feelable uses image AI to remake a picture for the fingers.

6
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:30,454
Touch a part of the example, and it tells you what it is.

7
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:39,213
There are ten ready-made lessons, five basic and five complex, from biology to maps.

8
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,670
Now, a photo of a real worksheet.

9
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:55,579
This is real processing, not sped up. Feelable reads every label, finds each part, and applies the rules for touch: raised outlines, a different texture for each neighbour, and braille.

10
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,689
Evaporation and rain become arrows you can follow with a finger.

11
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:13,419
Now the hard case: a textbook skull, with twenty-seven small labels, and bones of almost the same colour.

12
00:01:14,133 --> 00:01:24,782
Scan details shows exactly what the AI saw. Every label it read, every pointer it followed, and every bone split along a real edge. Even the sutures are traced where two bones meet.

13
00:01:28,633 --> 00:01:33,439
The teacher stays in charge. Anything uncertain is listed first, with a fix.

14
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:40,526
Missing a part? Click inside it, and Feelable suggests the outline. Students only see a lesson after the teacher approves it.

15
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:45,977
It understands structure, too.

16
00:01:46,567 --> 00:01:49,813
A flowchart is spoken with its yes and no branches.

17
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:53,334
And on a bus map, it works out where the routes meet.

18
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:01,099
Approve, and it's ready. Print it on swell paper, or share a QR code.

19
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:08,605
There's even a 3D printable relief of the same diagram.

20
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:23,646
And with no printer at all, a student explores on a phone.

21
00:02:24,633 --> 00:02:25,790
[phone] Nucleus.

22
00:02:26,633 --> 00:02:27,968
[phone] Nucleolus.

23
00:02:28,633 --> 00:02:29,918
[phone] Golgi body.

24
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:32,101
[phone] Mitochondrion.

25
00:02:32,683 --> 00:02:34,182
[phone] Cell membrane.

26
00:02:34,683 --> 00:02:36,016
[phone] Ribosomes.

27
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:40,141
[phone] Golgi body. Inside the cytoplasm.

28
00:02:41,633 --> 00:02:48,660
We measure where every outline lands. Valid targets rose from sixty-seven to a hundred and four, out of a hundred and forty-two.

29
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:51,862
Feelable. Every diagram, feelable.

